	Operational Value of BMS Trajectory Filtering
To conserve limited interceptor inventories during high-volume saturation attacks, the National Cognitive Battle Management System (BMS) executes automated trajectory filtering.


Using high-speed tracking arrays and processing algorithms, the BMS projects the precise ballistic or aerodynamic flight path of every detected incoming threat vector within seconds of launch. If the projected impact point falls within unpopulated terrain, open water, or non-critical acreage, the system flags the projectile as a non-threat and suppresses kinetic engagements. Operational modeling demonstrates that trajectory filtering yields a 60-75% reduction in total interceptor expenditure during complex saturation attacks, preserving critical magazine depth for protected assets.
Industrial Multiplier Effect
Federal capital expenditure on defense systems recirculates within the domestic economy. Advanced missile defense manufacturing relies on domestic supply chains for high-end microelectronics, specialized chemical processing, advanced metallurgy, and software development.


Synergizing defense procurement with broader industrial strategies—such as funding from the CHIPS and Science Act—accelerates domestic manufacturing capabilities:


1. Microelectronics & Edge Compute: Capital investments in military-grade microchip fabrication lines drive domestic commercial semiconductor manufacturing capacity.
2. Advanced Materials: Research into thermal protection systems for hypersonic defense yields commercial spin-offs in aerospace manufacturing, transportation, and industrial processing.
3. Radio Frequency & Sensing: Commercial satellite networks and civilian air-traffic infrastructure benefit from advancements in AESA radar technologies and optical sensor systems developed for defense applications.
